{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 1893 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "461d97117368b6123d1a454cc02c507cc22763bc19301d7311e9a9befaba9bd8" :edb/db-before-t 1892 :edb/db-after-t 1893 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535205 50 #inst "2026-10-08T19:30:42.203Z" 13194139535205 true] [17592186083122 1020 #edb/ref 17592186337451 13194139535205 true] [17592186083122 1020 #edb/ref 17592186337465 13194139535205 true] [17592186083213 1020 #edb/ref 17592186337458 13194139535205 true] [17592186083739 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535205 true] [17592186083739 1100 "Compute the arc length of $y = \\frac{2}{3}(x^{2} + 1)^{3/2}$ for $x ∈ [1, 4]$." 13194139535205 false] [17592186083739 1100 "Compute the arc length of $y=\\frac{2}{3}{({x}^{2}+1)}^{3/2}$ for $x\\in [1,4].$" 13194139535205 true] [17592186083739 1145 "The arc length of a curve $y=f(x)$ between $x=a$ and $x=b$ can be found using the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(x)=\\frac{2}{3}{({x}^{2}+1)}^{3/2},$ $a=1,$ and $b=4.$\n\nFirst, we find the derivative of $f(x):$\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot \\frac{2}{3}{({x}^{2}+1)}^{1/2}\\cdot 2x=2x\\sqrt{({x}^{2}+1)}\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute $\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}} & =\\sqrt{1+{[2x\\sqrt{({x}^{2}+1)}]}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+4{x}^{4}+4{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(2{x}^{2}+1)^{2}} \\\\  & =2{x}^{2}+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}=2{x}^{2}+1$ to the formula for the arc length, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}(2{x}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={(\\frac{2}{3}{x}^{3}+x)|}_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =[\\frac{2}{3}(4)^{3}+(4)]-[\\frac{2}{3}(1)^{3}+(1)] \\\\  & =45.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535205 true] [17592186337451 1017 #edb/ref 17592186337452 13194139535205 true] [17592186337451 1089 "q-122389" 13194139535205 true] [17592186337451 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535205 true] [17592186337451 1100 "The length of a curve $y=f(x)$ is given by ${\\int }_{1}^{4}\\sqrt{1+400{x}^{6}}\\,\\text{d}x.$ Which of the following could be  an equation for this curve?" 13194139535205 true] [17592186337451 1113 #uuid "64a47477-7e97-505b-9a22-72d4f75d41db" 13194139535205 true] [17592186337451 1145 "The arc length of a curve $y=f(x)$ between $x=a$ and $x=b$ can be found using the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe're given that \n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{1}^{4}\\sqrt{1+400{x}^{6}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nComparing the above with the arc length formula, we see that we must have\n\n$$\n[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}=400{x}^{6}\\,⟹\\,{f}^{′}(x)=\\pm 20{x}^{3}.\n$$\n\nIntegrating the above equation with respect to $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\pm 20\\int {x}^{3}\\text{ d}x \\\\  & =\\pm 5{x}^{4}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the given options, the only one that has the required form is $y=-5{x}^{4}+2.$" 13194139535205 true] [17592186337452 1000 #edb/ref 17592186337453 13194139535205 true] [17592186337452 1000 #edb/ref 17592186337454 13194139535205 true] [17592186337452 1000 #edb/ref 17592186337455 13194139535205 true] [17592186337452 1000 #edb/ref 17592186337456 13194139535205 true] [17592186337452 1000 #edb/ref 17592186337457 13194139535205 true] [17592186337452 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535205 true] [17592186337452 1041 "selection" 13194139535205 true] [17592186337452 1104 #edb/ref 17592186337457 13194139535205 true] [17592186337452 1110 #uuid "6bcee313-9598-5855-8424-1acba228c53f" 13194139535205 true] [17592186337453 1034 "y=-{x}^{4}+2" 13194139535205 true] [17592186337453 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535205 true] [17592186337453 1111 #uuid "b73479c4-84be-5da2-bded-e91dcfd7ee22" 13194139535205 true] [17592186337454 1034 "y=-\\frac{1}{5}{x}^{4}+2" 13194139535205 true] [17592186337454 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535205 true] [17592186337454 1111 #uuid "971dba84-487b-58a9-b1c1-8