/ref 17592186045465 13194139535205 true] [17592186337465 1100 "The length of a curve $y=f(x)$ is given by ${\\int }_{0}^{1/2}\\sqrt{1+\\frac{1}{(1-x)^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x.$ Which of the following could be  an equation for this curve?" 13194139535205 true] [17592186337465 1113 #uuid "8d968cc2-d41a-56be-9db0-c9b59a5e633c" 13194139535205 true] [17592186337465 1145 "The arc length of a curve $y=f(x)$ between $x=a$ and $x=b$ can be found using the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe're given that \n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{0}^{1/2}\\sqrt{1+\\frac{1}{(1-x)^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nComparing the above with the arc length formula, we see that we must have\n\n$$\n[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}=\\frac{1}{(1-x)^{2}}\\,⟹\\,{f}^{′}(x)=\\pm \\frac{1}{1-x}.\n$$\n\nIntegrating the above equation with respect to $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\pm \\int \\frac{1}{1-x}\\text{ d}x \\\\  & =\\pm (-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|1-x|)+C \\\\  & =\\pm \\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-x)+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the given options, the only one that has the required form is $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-x)+7.$" 13194139535205 true] [17592186337466 1000 #edb/ref 17592186337467 13194139535205 true] [17592186337466 1000 #edb/ref 17592186337468 13194139535205 true] [17592186337466 1000 #edb/ref 17592186337469 13194139535205 true] [17592186337466 1000 #edb/ref 17592186337470 13194139535205 true] [17592186337466 1000 #edb/ref 17592186337471 13194139535205 true] [17592186337466 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535205 true] [17592186337466 1041 "selection" 13194139535205 true] [17592186337466 1104 #edb/ref 17592186337471 13194139535205 true] [17592186337466 1110 #uuid "d9f9ce59-0774-5034-a0a7-9893cebb905f" 13194139535205 true] [17592186337467 1034 "y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)-1" 13194139535205 true] [17592186337467 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535205 true] [17592186337467 1111 #uuid "18433ded-ba7e-52ee-8921-d06888eea8bf" 13194139535205 true] [17592186337468 1034 "y=\\fra